  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are mycorrhizal fungi?",
      "a": "They are fungi that live in partnership with plant roots, wrapping them or growing into them. The word means fungus root. The plant gives sugar; the fungus gives water and minerals.",
      "asks": [
        "What does mycorrhiza mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do fungi give trees?",
      "a": "Water and minerals, especially phosphorus and nitrogen, gathered by threads that spread far beyond the roots. Some also help protect roots from disease.",
      "asks": [
        "How do fungi help plants grow?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do trees really talk through fungi?",
      "a": "Trees linked by fungi can exchange some carbon, water and chemical signals, but how much, and whether it truly helps neighbours, is still debated. Talking is a fun way of putting it, not a proven fact.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the wood wide web?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who discovered the wood wide web?",
      "a": "Mycorrhizal fungi have been known since the 1880s. In 1997 forest scientist Suzanne Simard reported carbon moving between birch and fir trees through fungal links, which made the idea famous.",
      "asks": [
        "Who is Suzanne Simard?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How common are mycorrhizal fungi?",
      "a": "Very common: roughly 80 to 90 percent of land plant species form partnerships with them. Fossils suggest plants and fungi teamed up when plants first moved onto land.",
      "asks": [
        "Do all plants have fungal partners?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The word mycorrhiza, meaning fungus root, was coined by Albert Frank in 1885.",
    "Roughly 80 to 90 percent of land plant species form partnerships with mycorrhizal fungi.",
    "In 1997 Suzanne Simard and colleagues reported carbon passing between birch and Douglas fir trees through fungal networks."
  ],
